WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.360
Reality TV can go light on the "reality" sometimes,
and presenting events as authentic and unaltered

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.570
while secretly scripting and staging important
scenes is common.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:15.530
Here are a few events that TV survivalist
Bear Grylls didn't really have to "survive,"

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.860
but it sure made for a better story onscreen.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.660
The Sierra Nevada mountain region is home
to some of the last truly wild horses in the

00:00:22.671 --> 00:00:26.700
United States, and wild mustangs have lived
in this area for centuries.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.570
In a Season 1 episode of Man Vs. Wild, Grylls
was shown attempting to lasso some of the

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:37.161
steeds, but it turned out that these mustangs
might not have been so wild after all, the

00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:41.700
crew reportedly "hired" them from a trekking
station near their filming location.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.960
These were actually relatively tame animals
that were accustomed to being around humans,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.600
according to a Sunday Times of London report.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.140
Of course, that doesn't play as well to audiences.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.060
What makes for a more interesting scene: showing
Grylls trying to sling a lasso around a "wild"

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.450
mustang, or admitting that the crew essentially
rented a few horses that weren't actually

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:02.450
running wild?

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.320
The answer is obvious.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:09.420
Everyone knows that an active volcano poses
a threat because of all the lava, and there

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.080
are other dangers that can be present, like
toxic gas.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.740
In an episode of Man vs. Wild in which Grylls
explores the Mount Kilauea volcano in Hawaii,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:24.369
he's shown working to avoid patches of molten
lava and clouds of so-called "killer" gas.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:28.640
But a safety adviser for the show told the
Sunday Times that, while the gas should be

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.540
avoided in large quantities, the drama on
the show was heightened using off-camera smoke

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.560
machines.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.420
As for the lava itself?

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.100
The crew used burning coals to make the scene
look more dangerous.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.660
An active volcano is scary enough, they probably
didn't have to add all that.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.060
In Man vs. Wild, Grylls is supposed to be
on his own in whatever inhospitable environment

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.210
he ended up in.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:57.720
After all, it's called Man vs. Wild, not "Grylls
and his crew lending a helping hand vs. Wild."

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.759
But it's been revealed that Grylls has been
assisted by his crew on several occasions

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:06.800
without letting the audience in on the truth,
even when he was not in immediate danger.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.160
"I'm getting dive-bombed by these seagulls!"

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.320
After serving as a consultant on the show,
survival expert Mark Weinert told the Times,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.560
For instance, in one episode that Weinert
worked on, Grylls supposedly built a raft

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.760
using only natural materials that he found
around him, like hibiscus twine, palm leaves,

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:31.760
and bamboo.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.180
However, Weinert said that he personally worked
with a team of builders to put the raft together;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.480
then it was taken apart so Grylls could be
filmed putting it together on camera.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.620
In addition to Mark Weinert, Grylls and his
crew often brought on other survival experts

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.160
as consultants on Man vs. Wild.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.460
There was nothing shady about that in particular,
as long as they weren't doing Grylls' work

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:53.140
for him.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.660
But on at least one occasion, a consultant
later revealed that this was exactly what

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:58.750
was going on.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:03.879
Nick Vroomans worked with Grylls on an episode
of Man vs. Wild in western Australia.

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:07.989
Vroomans told The Herald Sun that while he
enjoyed his experience working on the show

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:12.020
and thought that Grylls was a good person
overall, viewers should know that the show

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.019
was predominantly scripted.

00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:18.040
Vroomans also explained that he was often
responsible for building Grylls' shelters

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.220
and finding him animals to eat; in this episode,
it was snakes.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.220
Admitting he was disappointed by the final
cut of the episode, Vroomans added,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.240
Les Stroud is the host of the show Survivorman,
which had a similar premise to Man vs. Wild,

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:40.440
but Stroud insists that his show was a much
more realistic picture of what it's like to

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.300
survive in the wild, and Man vs. Wild was
basically a sham.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:51.590
Stroud is not a big fan of Grylls, and he
made this very clear in a Reddit AMA in 2013.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:55.239
He stated that many of the survival skills
that Grylls had "taught" on the show would

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:57.440
actually not be very helpful.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.650
For example, Stroud singled out an episode
in which Grylls was shown squeezing drinking

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:06.050
water from elephant dung, something Stroud
claims would be impossible unless the crew

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.780
specifically soaked it with water beforehand.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.180
"That is a first."

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:11.620
Moral of the story?

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:17.019
Man vs. Wild shouldn't be seen as a realistic,
documentary-style show; it's entertainment

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:18.380
above anything else.

